==================================================
PHASE 8 GOALS
==================================================

Make the website:

1. Easy for Google to crawl
2. Easy for Google to understand
3. Easy for users to navigate between related academic pages
4. Free from accidental duplicate URLs
5. Properly indexed page-by-page
6. Structured around the academic hierarchy

==================================================
PART 1 — AUDIT CURRENT SEO
==================================================

Before changing anything, inspect the existing implementation.

Identify:

- metadata implementation
- generateMetadata()
- sitemap implementation
- robots implementation
- canonical implementation
- Open Graph implementation
- Twitter metadata
- JSON-LD / structured data
- breadcrumb implementation
- heading structure
- internal linking
- noindex/nofollow usage

DO NOT immediately rewrite existing SEO.

First determine what already exists.

==================================================
PART 2 — URL CANONICALIZATION
==================================================

Every indexable page must have ONE canonical URL.

Canonical URLs should follow the existing public route structure.

Examples:

https://ai-ml-notes-wine.vercel.app/notes

https://ai-ml-notes-wine.vercel.app/notes/btech

https://ai-ml-notes-wine.vercel.app/notes/btech/cse

https://ai-ml-notes-wine.vercel.app/notes/btech/cse/machine-learning

Do NOT include tracking parameters in canonical URLs.

For example:

?utm_source=chatgpt.com

must NEVER become the canonical URL.

Do not create duplicate canonical variants.

==================================================
PART 3 — TITLE TAGS
==================================================

Every indexable page needs a unique SEO title.

Do not use the same title template blindly for every page.

Recommended patterns:

HOME:

AI & ML Notes | Exam-Focused BTech & MTech Study Notes

NOTES:

AI & ML Notes | BTech & MTech Exam Notes

COURSE:

BTech Notes | AI & ML Exam Notes

MTech Notes | AI & ML Exam Notes

BRANCH:

BTech CSE Notes | Computer Science Engineering

MTech CSE Notes | Computer Science Engineering

SUBJECT:

Machine Learning Notes for BTech CSE | Unit-wise Exam Notes

TOPIC:

Decision Tree in Machine Learning | BTech CSE Notes

Use actual metadata.

Do not invent branch/course/subject information.

==================================================
PART 4 — META DESCRIPTIONS
==================================================

Every important indexable page needs a unique description.

Descriptions should:

- describe the actual page
- mention relevant academic context
- be useful to a student
- avoid keyword stuffing

Example:

Machine Learning notes for BTech CSE covering unit-wise concepts, 2-mark, 5-mark and 10-mark exam preparation, examples and quick revision.

Do not copy the same description across all pages.

Generate descriptions from real page metadata/content where possible.

==================================================
PART 5 — H1 / HEADING STRUCTURE
==================================================

Every indexable page must have exactly ONE primary H1.

Recommended:

Course page:

# BTech Notes

Branch page:

# BTech CSE Notes

Subject page:

# Machine Learning Notes

Topic page:

# Decision Tree in Machine Learning

Use H2 for major sections.

Use H3 for subsections.

Do not use headings purely for visual styling.

==================================================
PART 6 — BREADCRUMB STRUCTURED DATA
==================================================

Keep the existing visible breadcrumbs.

Add JSON-LD BreadcrumbList structured data where appropriate.

Example hierarchy:

Home
→ Notes
→ BTech
→ CSE
→ Machine Learning
→ Decision Tree

Every breadcrumb item should point to a real canonical internal URL.

Do not create fake breadcrumb URLs.

==================================================
PART 7 — ARTICLE / EDUCATIONAL STRUCTURED DATA
==================================================

For topic pages, evaluate whether Article or EducationalContent structured data is appropriate.

Use only schema types that accurately represent the page.

Do NOT add fake:

- ratings
- reviews
- author credentials
- publication dates
- organization claims

If Article structured data is used, use real values from metadata.

Do not create misleading structured data purely to improve rankings.

==================================================
PART 8 — ORGANIZATION / WEBSITE SCHEMA
==================================================

